# Silent failure is a bug (anti-flake clause 1)

[`no-panic.mdc`](no-panic.mdc) covers the panic case: every recoverable
failure propagates via `Result` rather than aborting. But "propagate" is
not "log". The post-2026-05-19 win32 configurator audit caught call sites
where a daemon's inner task returned `Err(_)`, the caller forwarded it up
another level, and three levels later it landed in a top-level `select!`
arm that dropped it with `Err(_) => {}`. The daemon kept running. The
sync silently stopped. The configurator showed "last sync: 6 hours ago"
with no failure indication.

## The rule

> **Every reachable `Err(_)` arm in production code logs through
> `tracing` (`warn!` for transient, `error!` for definitive) with the
> operation name, the relevant inputs (privacy-appropriate, secrets
> masked per
> [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §5), the error
> chain (wrapped via `anyhow::Context` or a typed `thiserror` enum), and
> the recovery action taken. An early `return Err(e);` without a paired
> `tracing::warn!` / `tracing::error!` at or above the originating site
> is a bug.**

"Caller will log it" is not acceptable. Errors lose context at each `?`
boundary; by the time the error reaches the top of the stack the original
op name and inputs are usually gone. Log at the **originating site** (the
place that knows the op name and inputs) AND let the error propagate. The
top-level handler logs the propagated chain again for correlation — the
two records answer different questions ("what failed" vs "how did it
unwind").

## Required log shape

Structured fields, not message interpolation, so the record is searchable
in Loki / Splunk / Datadog and correlates with the Windows Event Log
entries that use the same field names:

```rust
tracing::warn!(
    op = "snake_case_op_name",
    target_kind = "tenant",           // or "agent", "endpoint", "settings_file"
    target = %target_name_or_id_or_url_redacted,
    error = format!("{:#}", err),      // full anyhow chain
    recovery = "retry|skip|pause|abort_and_unhealth",
    "human-readable summary in US English",
);
```

The **recovery action is named in every record**, so an operator reading
the rolling log can answer "is the daemon trying again?" without source
access.

## Forbidden constructs

- `Err(_) => {}` / `let _ = future.await;` on a non-trivial future. Match
  on the specific error variant and log.
- `return Err(e);` without a `tracing::warn!` / `tracing::error!` at the
  same site (outside `#[cfg(test)]`). The single exception is when the
  function name itself is the op name and the immediate caller has its
  own log site that knows the inputs.
- A `Result<_, _>` returned from a top-level `tokio::spawn` task body
  with no outer wrapper that catches the result and logs on `Err`. A
  spawned task whose `JoinHandle` is dropped swallows panics **and**
  `Err` returns silently.
- `tracing::debug!` / `trace!` as the only log on a reachable error path.
  Errors are either `warn!` (transient — retry will likely succeed) or
  `error!` (definitive — manual intervention needed).
- A `tracing::error!` followed by `Ok(())` "to avoid breaking the loop".
  If it was definitive enough to log at `error!`, the loop must reflect
  the failure in its return type, its state machine, or its
  `runtime_status.json` fingerprint — not silently continue.
- `.map_err(|_| MyError::Something)` discarding the source. Always
  preserve it: `.map_err(|e| MyError::Something { source: e.into() })` or
  `.context("doing X")?`.

## Worked violation → fix — the scheduler's HTTP loop

**Violation** — `sync_one_cycle` returns a rich error variant; the loop
discards it:

```rust
_ = ticker.tick() => {
    if let Err(_) = sync_one_cycle(&http, &state).await {
        state.consecutive_failures += 1;
    }
}
```

The configurator shows `consecutive_failures = 47` six hours later and
the operator has no idea why. The Windows Event Log has nothing. Loki
shows nothing. The log file shows nothing above `debug`.

**Fix** — match each variant, name the recovery, and make the failure
visible in `runtime_status.json`:

```rust
_ = ticker.tick() => {
    match sync_one_cycle(&http, &state).await {
        Ok(outcome) => {
            tracing::info!(op = "sync_loop", records = outcome.records,
                elapsed_ms = outcome.elapsed.as_millis() as u64, "sync cycle complete");
            state.consecutive_failures = 0;
            state.last_success = Some(OffsetDateTime::now_utc());
        }
        Err(SyncError::Transport(msg)) => {
            state.consecutive_failures += 1;
            tracing::warn!(
                op = "sync_loop",
                consecutive_failures = state.consecutive_failures,
                error = %msg,
                recovery = if state.consecutive_failures < MAX_FAILURES {
                    "retry on next tick"
                } else {
                    "pausing scheduler; mark unhealthy"
                },
                "sync cycle failed (transport)",
            );
            if state.consecutive_failures >= MAX_FAILURES {
                runtime_status::mark_unhealthy("sync", &msg);
                scheduler::pause(&shutdown).await;
            }
        }
        Err(SyncError::Unhandled(msg)) => {
            tracing::error!(op = "sync_loop", error = %msg,
                recovery = "pause scheduler; require operator intervention",
                "sync cycle failed with unhandled error");
            runtime_status::mark_unhealthy("sync", &msg);
            scheduler::pause(&shutdown).await;
        }
    }
}
```

The operator's "last sync 6 hours ago" question is now answerable from
the rolling log alone, and the configurator badge and Windows Event Log
reflect the unhealthy state.

